{"entryType":"UniProtKB reviewed (Swiss-Prot)","primaryAccession":"Q3YL96","uniProtkbId":"CDIA_ECOLX","entryAudit":{"firstPublicDate":"2015-03-04","lastAnnotationUpdateDate":"2026-09-02","lastSequenceUpdateDate":"2005-09-27","entryVersion":78,"sequenceVersion":1},"annotationScore":4.0,"organism":{"scientificName":"Escherichia coli","taxonId":562,"lineage":["Bacteria","Pseudomonadati","Pseudomonadota","Gammaproteobacteria","Enterobacterales","Enterobacteriaceae","Escherichia"]},"proteinExistence":"1: Evidence at protein level","proteinDescription":{"recommendedName":{"fullName":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000303","source":"PubMed","id":"16109881"}],"value":"Toxin CdiA"}},"alternativeNames":[{"fullName":{"value":"CdiA-EC93"}}],"flag":"Precursor"},"genes":[{"geneName":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000303","source":"PubMed","id":"16109881"}],"value":"cdiA"}}],"comments":[{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"16109881"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"19124575"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"21085179"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"23882017"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"25174572"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"Reference","id":"Ref.6"}],"value":"Toxic component of a toxin-immunity protein module, which functions as a cellular contact-dependent growth inhibition (CDI) system. CDI modules allow bacteria to communicate with and inhibit the growth of closely related neighboring bacteria (target cell counts decrease 1000- to 10(5)-fold) in a contact-dependent fashion. Inhibitory cells must be in logarithmic (not stationary) phase to inhibit growth of their targets, but protein synthesis is not necessary (PubMed:16109881, PubMed:25174572). The presence of P or S but not type 1 pili protects the target cells against growth inhibition for this CDI. BamA on the outer membrane of target cells acts as a receptor for CdiA, while target cell multidrug efflux pump AcrB facilitates its transport into the cytoplasm (PubMed:16109881). Outer membrane receptor function is dependent on extracellular loops of BamA (PubMed:23882017). Cells undergoing CDI show a 2- to 5-fold reversible decrease in aerobic respiration, proton motive force and steady-state ATP levels, suggesting this CT module is an ionophore that disrupts the target cell's inner cell membrane. Growth recovery requires an energy source. Cells expressing this protein in the absence of CdiI initially form filaments, some of which contain multiple nucleoids, while others are devoid of nucleoids. CDI cells induce the phage shock response, but pspA is not required for recovery from CDI (PubMed:19124575). CDI is neutralized by its cognate immunity protein CdiI, but not by non-cognate CdiI from other bacteria with different CDI systems (PubMed:21085179). Plays a role in biofilm formation, a region N-terminal to residue 644 is implicated in this receptor-independent cell adhesion (Ref.6)"}],"commentType":"FUNCTION"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"30388452"}],"value":"The CdiA protein is thought to be exported from the cell through the central lumen of CdiB, the other half of its two-partner system (TPS). The TPS domain probably remains associated with CdiB while the FHA-1 domain forms an extended filament (33 nm long) with the receptor-binding domain (RBD) at its extremity; in the secretion arrested state the C-terminus of the RBD and YP domains form a hairpin-like structure as the FHA-2, PT and CT domains are periplasmic. The YP domain is probably responsible for this arrest at the point where it re-enters the host cell periplasm. Upon binding to a target cell outer membrane receptor (BamA for this CDI) a signal is transmitted to activate secretion. The filament becomes about 5 nm longer, the rest of CdiA is secreted and the FHA-2 domain becomes stably associated with the target cell's outer membrane where it facilitates entry of the toxic CT domain into the target cell periplasm. From there the toxic CT domain is cleaved and gains access to the target cell cytoplasm via an inner membrane protein (multidrug efflux pump AcrB for this CDI)"}],"commentType":"FUNCTION"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"23882017"}],"value":"Probably interacts with cognate immunity protein CdiI"}],"commentType":"SUBUNIT"},{"commentType":"SUBCELLULAR LOCATION","note":{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"25174572"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"30388452"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305"}],"value":"Secreted to the cell surface by CdiB, its two partner secretion pathway (TPS) partner (Probable). Toxin translocation into the target cell depends on the proton motive force of the target cell, but not on tolA or tonB (PubMed:25174572). Forms multiple filaments that extend away from the host cell surface, averaging 33 nm long; protein truncated at residue 1929 still forms filaments (PubMed:30388452)"}]},"subcellularLocations":[{"location":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000303","source":"PubMed","id":"28351921"}],"value":"Secreted","id":"SL-0243"}},{"location":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"25174572"}],"value":"Target cell, target cell cytoplasm","id":"SL-0486"}}]},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"21085179"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"28351921"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"30388452"}],"value":"The CDI activity resides in the approximately 230 residue C-terminal (CT) domain; exchanging the C-terminal (CT) domain and cdiI gene between different strains confers resistance within cognate but not non-cognate systems (i.e. CdiI-EC93 neutralizes CdiA-CT from strain EC93 but not CdiA-CT from E.coli strain 536 / UPEC, Y.pestis strain CO92 or D.dadantii strain 3937). Intracellular expression of the last 223 residues of CdiA-EC93 inhibits growth, which can be relieved by CdiI from strain EC93 but not 536 / UPEC (PubMed:21085179). Exchanging the outer membrane receptor-binding domains between proteins exchanges the receptors recognized by CdiA (PubMed:28351921). Extracellular filaments are formed by the N-terminus of the protein (residues 1-1929) (PubMed:30388452)"}],"commentType":"DOMAIN"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"16109881"}],"value":"Expressed as 303 kDa protein which can be processed to 284 kDa and 195 kDa forms"}],"commentType":"PTM"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"16109881"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"21085179"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"Reference","id":"Ref.6"}],"value":"Loss of contact-dependent growth inhibition (PubMed:16109881). In competition experiments between EC93 cells encoding or deleted for cdiA and cdiI, cdiA/cdiI minus cells were quickly outcompeted by wild-type, probably due to intraspecies CDI (PubMed:21085179). Decreased biofilm formation (Ref.6)"}],"commentType":"DISRUPTION PHENOTYPE"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305"}],"value":"In the N-terminal section; belongs to the CdiA toxin family"}],"commentType":"SIMILARITY"}],"features":[{"type":"Signal","location":{"start":{"value":1,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":32,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"Signal","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000303","source":"PubMed","id":"28351921"}]},{"type":"Chain","location":{"start":{"value":33,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":3132,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"Toxin CdiA","featureId":"PRO_0000432089"},{"type":"Region","location":{"start":{"value":36,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":322,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"Two-partner system transport domain (TPS)","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"Reference","id":"Ref.6"}]},{"type":"Region","location":{"start":{"value":351,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":1378,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"FHA-1","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"30388452"}]},{"type":"Region","location":{"start":{"value":1379,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":1635,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"Receptor-binding domain (RBD)","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"28351921"}]},{"type":"Region","location":{"start":{"value":1636,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":1820,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"YP domain","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"30388452"}]},{"type":"Region","location":{"start":{"value":1821,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":1859,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"Periplasmic FHA-1 repeat (pFR)","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"30388452"}]},{"type":"Region","location":{"start":{"value":1930,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":2526,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"FHA-2","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"28351921"}]},{"type":"Region","location":{"start":{"value":2195,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":2228,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"Disordered","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000256","source":"SAM","id":"MobiDB-lite"}]},{"type":"Region","location":{"start":{"value":2456,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":2497,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"Disordered","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000256","source":"SAM","id":"MobiDB-lite"}]},{"type":"Region","location":{"start":{"value":2862,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":2904,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"Pre-toxin (PT) domain","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"28351921"}]},{"type":"Region","location":{"start":{"value":2909,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":3121,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"C-terminal effector domain (CT)","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305",